00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:07.299 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  Circle Circle dot dot you just got the  cootie shot that was a day my friend  Amanda saved me in the first grade  because by giving me this cootie shot  which is just like a simple vaccination  she effectively made me immune to a  crazy disease called the cooties which  for those of you who don't know is this  highly contagious and infectious disease  that's transmitted by little boys and  girls when they talk to each other in  elementary school so for a  seven-year-old Mexican girl who had just  arrived to the u.s. it kind of took me  some time to understand why the girls in  my class that I had gotten the cooties  to begin with see every day during lunch  I did something outrageous because I  would go out into the playground with my  lunchbox and I would just talk to my  best friend Alonso Alonso sorry Zeke and  that somehow required this vaccination  that cootie shot stuck with me literally  until today fast forward a couple of  decades and Here I am at Stanford  excited to talk about gender equality  issues a subject I care deeply about  since that pretty traumatizing day at  the playground except every time we talk  about gender equality issues everything  from gender biases equal pay gender role  expectations women in the workplace  all I see around me is for the most part  women women participants and women  panelists discussing what it means to be  a woman and when we do engage with men  in these conversations perhaps in  classrooms like this one or forums or  dinners I kind of get a sense that the  air starts to become a little bit  thicker that the tension rises and that  the conversations don't seem to move  forward and that brings me to the  subject of today's low-key note small  conversations can create big changes  I'll walk you through three things today  first so what goes on when we have these  conversations between men and women that  makes things so difficult  second why does it matter to have these  conversations anyways I'm sorry what can  we do to make things better so on the  first point what goes on when we engage  in these conversations that makes things  so difficult to explore that I surveyed  135 men and 131 women at the GSP that's  more than one out of every four of you  anonymously and had coffee chats with  over 30 men over the past month where I  simply asked you what do you think and  how do you feel when we engage in these  conversations with the GSB and what I  found was quite unsettling I'll start  first with what I found out about the  men see the men they're not feeling  great in these conversations more than  70% of men describe their feelings as  frustrated and not excited more than a  third said their first reaction when  they found themselves in these  conversations was oh no one of them  changed the multiple choice option and  said my first reaction is to find the  nearest exit and see that might be  because men are feeling judged men said  things like I'm feeling guilty for just  showing up as a man in these  conversations it's a little loose for me  so easy to mess it up I just rather not  say anything if I make the slightest  mistake I'll be vilified regardless of  what I say women still get offended the  downside is pretty high the upside not  clear so not surprisingly 81% of men  said they tend to participate less when  talking about gender equality than any  other issue at the GSB but see despite  not feeling great and feeling judged men  are supportive ninety-six percent of men  said the gender equality issues are  important to them and that they should  be discussed many of them quoted their  wives or sisters their daughters their  partners saying things like I love my  wife  but that doesn't give me a say in these  conversations while I'm wildly  supportive there's something insincere  about my opinion I'm a strong supporter  of gender equality but I find myself in  the way of gender equality so the  frustration comes it seems from a place  of being easily misunderstood not from a  place of not caring so what did I find  out  about the women see the women are also  not feeling great in these conversations  more than 75% of women describe their  feelings as frustrated too and defensive  and their frustration comes from  different reasons in the men it's  because these conversations don't move  quickly we stay on the same safe and  superficial topics it's hard to get my  point across the men don't get it and  women make it very personal the majority  of women described a big incentive to  participate saying things like the  majority of women described a big  incentive to participate saying things  like I have to go out there and defend  my gender I'm carrying the torch I'm  paving the road and see I can speak for  myself and saying that it's there's so  much in my mind about these topics that  it is very easy to get very emotional  and charged up about it as I just did  now so zooming out from what I found out  about the survey about the men and the  women this kind of reminded me of the  game of hide-and-seek see to me the  women are playing seekers it's our  territory we're on the lookout looking  for any small mistake and the men are  kind of playing hiding they feel a lil  incentive to participate they're kind of  waiting for the game to be over and they  can find the nearest exit but the  problem with this dynamic is that we've  missed each other in these conversations  we've missed the opportunity to build  something of value to innovate to share  personal stories to actually hear what  the other person has to say small  conversations can create big changes so  now to my second point why does it  matter that we can have good  conversations anyways for inspiration  for that I went to a place very far  removed from the GSB growing up I  visited an indigenous community in the  northern mountains of Mexico where  gender equality issues are pretty real  and for years in this community the  little girls didn't have access to  schools because there were only enough  teachers to educate the little boys but  you see all of the teachers were women  so these women teachers gathered  together and door by door and family by  family had the smallest of conversations  with these families to explain that if  they didn't educate the little girls  there wouldn't be any teachers for the  little boys or girls of generations to  come and the point of the story is that  it was because of having these small  conversations and stepping into the  homes of these families that they were  able to create bigger changes that  reminds me of the story of one of the  men I served  ESP who talked to me about his mom who  had been abandoned by her husband and  had to quit school at a very young age  and worked tirelessly her entire life to  get her son to Stanford and he said the  most productive conversations I have  about gender equality issues at the GSB  are when I Skype with my mom on the  weekends so not in these classrooms and  that really got to me see because the  point about being good at these  conversations isn't to talk about me and  my extremely privileged position as a  woman at the GSB but rather to give a  space and voice to talk about the  millions of women who can't even dream  of the possibility of being at the GSB  let alone have nine minutes to talk to  you about talking about it so now to my  third point what can we do to make  things better my gift to you today is  the following Circle Circle dot dot you  just got a cootie shot you see a man who  never told me that the cooties requires  a revaccination in grad school and the  point of that is that we should feel  free to go out there and have  conversations that make us feel excited  engaged and not frustrated and that  requires a lot of intentionality on both  the men and the women  it requires suspending judgment making  mistakes allowing the other to make  mistakes see over the past month I've  learned more about gender equality by  talking to men and asking men than I  ever learned in any woman only event and  I realize that as a person that gets  very emotional and takes this topic very  personally I have an important choice to  make about how I approach men in these  conversations I can choose to get easily  offended or I can choose to get easily  curious I choose to get easily curious  so I want you to pick a person from the  opposite gender in this room I pick  Alonzo and invite that person for a  15-minute coffee chat about gender  equality and if you don't have a  specific topic in mind you can talk  about your reactions to this so keynote  and the challenge here is for the men  ask the uncomfortable question and feel  free to say what you actually think and  to the women ask them what they think  and how they feel and be prepared to get  curious and maybe instead of playing a  game of hide-and-seek we can play a game  of puzzles where we put the pieces  together bit by bit because I do think  that it's in these small conversations  between the little boy  girls in that playground in elementary  school and between the young men and  women here now at the GSB where we'll  find the answers to those bigger  questions thank you  [Applause]  [Music]

